    "title": "THAT ISLAND FEELING, MON",
    "text": "Thursday, July 23, 2026\nAlthea Alquitran, depicting Disney princess Moana, sings and dances with children Monday at the Jamestown Philomenian Library. The event for students in grades K-4 was an upbeat interactive program with “Polynesian fusion.” Photos by Andrea von Hohenleiten\nAlquitran encapsulates Moana, whose character in the 2016 animation was the strong-willed daughter of a village chief who was chosen by the ocean to restore a mystical relic to the goddess Te Fiti.\nKids learn to Hula dance with Moana.\nJoshua Cordtz, left, and Julian Beauchamp, both 6, limbo the easy way.\nDaisey Ryder, 7, left, and Eliza Comley sing along with Moana.\nYuna Johnston, 8, and her 8-month-old sister, Megumi, pose with the Polynesian princess.\nEliza Comley, 8, Zoe Loera, 7, and Riley Loera, 5, make Hula motions referring to nature, the ocean and love.",
    "summary": "Althea Alquitran, depicting Disney princess Moana, sings and dances with children Monday at the Jamestown Philomenian Library. The event for students in grades K-4 was an upbeat interactive program with “Polynesian fusion.” Photos by Andrea von Hohenleiten Alquitran encapsulates Moana, whose character in the 2016 animation was the strong-willed daughter of a village chief who was chosen by the ocean to restore a mystical relic to the goddess Te Fiti. Joshua Cordtz, left, and Julian Beauchamp, both 6, limbo the easy way. Daisey Ryder, 7, left, and Eliza Comley sing along with Moana.",
